ments in the same group are the\nfirst associates and two treatments in the different groups are the second associates. This is called the\ngroup divisible type scheme. The scheme simply amounts to arrange the v  pq treatments in a\npq rectangle and then the association scheme can be exhibited. The columns in the pq\nrectangle will form the groups.\nUnder this association scheme,\nn q1\n1\nn q(p1),\n2\nhence\n(q1)q(p1) r(k1)\n1 2\nand the parameters of the second kind are uniquely determined by p and q. In this case\nq2 0  0 q1 \nP  , P  ,\n   \n1 0 q(p1) 2 q1 q(p2)\nFor every group divisible design,\nr ,\n1\nrkv 0.\n2\nAnalysis of Variance | Chapter 7 | Partial. BIBD | Shalabh, IIT Kanpur\n1144\n\nIf r , then the group divisible design is said to be singular. Such a singular group divisible design\n1\ncan always be derived from a corresponding BIBD. To obtain this, just replace each treatment by a\ngroup of q treatments. In general, if a BIBD has the following parameters b*,v*,r*,k*,*, then a\ngroup divisible design is obtained from this BIBD which has parameters\nbb*, vqv*, r r*, k qk*, r,  *, n  p, n q.\n1 2 1 2\nA group divisible design is nonsingular if r . Nonsingular group divisible designs can be divided\n1\ninto two classes – semi-regular and regular.\nA group divisible design is said to be semi-regular if r  and rkv 0. For this design\n1 2\nb v p1.\nAlso, each block contains the same number of treatments from each group so that k must be divisible\nby p.\nA group divisible design is said to be regular if r  and rkv 0. For this design\n1 2\nb  v.\nLatin Square Type Association Scheme\nLet L denotes the Latin square type PBIBD with i constraints. In this PBIBD, the number of\ni\ntreatments are expressible as vq2. The treatments may be arranged in a qq square. For the\ncase of i2, two treatments are the first associates if they occur in the same row or the same\ncolumn, and second as